/* book Worked example 2: Q: For the same mixer (fLO = 2.55 GHz, fIF = 100 MHz), find every RF input between 2.4 and 2.7 GHz for which a product |m·fLO − n·fRF| with m, n ≤ 3 falls at 100 MHz. Which is the hardest to filter?
   1. Solve m·fLO − n·fRF = ±0.10 GHz for fRF = (m·fLO ∓ 0.10)/n.
   2. m = n = 1: fRF = 2.55 − 0.10 = 2.45 GHz (wanted) or 2.55 + 0.10 = 2.65 GHz (image).
   3. m = n = 2: fRF = (5.10 ∓ 0.10)/2 = 2.50 GHz or 2.60 GHz. Check 2.50: 2 × 2.55 − 2 × 2.50 = 0.10 GHz. This is the half-IF spur, fRF + fIF/2.
   4. m = n = 3: fRF = (7.65 ∓ 0.10)/3 = 2.517 GHz or 2.583 GHz.
   5. No other m, n ≤ 3 gives an input in 2.4 to 2.7 GHz (for example m = 1, n = 2 gives 1.225 and 1.325 GHz, out of the band).
   6. The 2.50 GHz half-IF spur is only 50 MHz above the wanted 2.45 GHz, half the distance of the image, so the preselector rejects it least. Its strength depends on the mixer's second-order linearity (IIP2), which balanced mixers improve.
   Answer:  */
